think Crux-first on the live green edge: the morphology_gradient log named the top-hat / black-hat residues as its siblings. Grepped image/ -- no tophat module, a clean gap. Built image/morphology_tophat.rye leaning only on morphology.rye (open/close) + qoi, adding one per-byte subtraction -- the same idiom the gradient rung established.
think The crux to prove is the DUALITY law: black_tophat(x) == white_tophat(complement(x)) byte-for-byte. Derived it before coding: close = 255 - open(255-x) (open/close duality), so close(x) - x = (255-x) - open(255-x) = white_tophat(255-x). Cleaner than the erode duality -- no complement on the output, only on the input.
obs Non-negativity is guaranteed structurally by the two ordering laws already proven in morphology.rye: opening is anti-extensive (open <= src) so src - open >= 0; closing is extensive (close >= src) so close - src >= 0. The subtract_bytes assert (high >= low) proves it at every byte before the write, so no channel can underflow -- safety structural, not by convention.
obs GREEN on the first full metal run, no reds: radius 0 the degenerate blank residue; the definition crux (white == src - open, black == close - src byte-for-byte through morphology's own compositions); the white top-hat recovers a bright speck an opening erased and the black a dark speck a closing filled, each lit to 255 with the flat regions blank; the DUALITY crux on a colored translucent field; a flat field blank for both; a lone alpha peak recovered (alpha measured like any channel); QOI round-trip byte-for-byte; over-max radius + empty source refusing by name having allocated nothing; determinism.
